From 8ba8477183dfd981cccd319e8ad6462a36a21dff Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Gus Bourg <gus@bourg.net>
Date: Wed, 2 Sep 2026 17:30:00 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] media: meson: vdec: re-pulse decode pipeline resets after
 power-up for h264

The only reset the session power-on path issues (DOS_SW_RESET0 =
0xfffffffc in vdec_1_start) runs while DOS_MEM_PD_VDEC is still
all-ones and the power island is still isolated, so the write-back /
deblock pipeline state around the just-power-cycled SRAMs never sees
an effective reset.  The result: the first GOP of every session
decodes with non-deterministically corrupt luma (chroma untouched,
~28dB PSNR-y, different output on every run) until the next IDR
resets the prediction chain.

Mirror the vendor vh264_prot_init() sequence: after firmware load,
with clocks running and memories un-gated, pulse VLD_PART/IQIDCT/MC
twice and then DBLK/PIC_DC (DOS_SW_RESET0 bits 4,6,7 then 8,9 - the
AMRISC cpus, bits 11/12, are untouched so the loaded IMEM survives).

With this, H.264 hardware decode is bit-exact against software for
every frame of every session including the first GOP, and the output
is deterministic across sessions (identical md5 sets, 1080p and 4K).
---
NOT SUBMITTED. This patch is written in kernel submission form so that it
can be sent as-is, but as of publication it has not been posted to linux-media,
has had no upstream review, and is carried out-of-tree by the Khadas VIM3
EDK2 port. The Signed-off-by below is a DCO attestation of origin, not a
claim that this went anywhere.

 drivers/staging/media/meson/vdec/codec_h264.c | 24 +++++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 24 insertions(+)

diff --git a/drivers/staging/media/meson/vdec/codec_h264.c b/drivers/staging/media/meson/vdec/codec_h264.c
index 89e0f86..b912b13 100644
--- a/drivers/staging/media/meson/vdec/codec_h264.c
+++ b/drivers/staging/media/meson/vdec/codec_h264.c
@@ -182,6 +182,30 @@ static int codec_h264_start(struct amvdec_session *sess)
 	if (!h264->sei_vaddr)
 		return -ENOMEM;
 
+	/*
+	 * Re-pulse the decode-pipeline resets (VLD_PART, IQIDCT, MC, then
+	 * DBLK, PIC_DC) now that the clocks are running and the DOS
+	 * memories are un-gated.  The reset issued during power-on ran
+	 * while DOS_MEM_PD_VDEC was still all-ones and the power island
+	 * isolated, so the write-back/deblock state around the
+	 * just-power-cycled SRAMs never saw an effective reset - the
+	 * first GOP of every session decodes with non-deterministically
+	 * corrupt luma until the next IDR.  Mirrors the vendor
+	 * vh264_prot_init() sequence.
+	 */
+	amvdec_write_dos(core, DOS_SW_RESET0, BIT(7) | BIT(6) | BIT(4));
+	amvdec_write_dos(core, DOS_SW_RESET0, 0);
+	amvdec_read_dos(core, DOS_SW_RESET0);
+	amvdec_read_dos(core, DOS_SW_RESET0);
+	amvdec_read_dos(core, DOS_SW_RESET0);
+	amvdec_write_dos(core, DOS_SW_RESET0, BIT(7) | BIT(6) | BIT(4));
+	amvdec_write_dos(core, DOS_SW_RESET0, 0);
+	amvdec_write_dos(core, DOS_SW_RESET0, BIT(9) | BIT(8));
+	amvdec_write_dos(core, DOS_SW_RESET0, 0);
+	amvdec_read_dos(core, DOS_SW_RESET0);
+	amvdec_read_dos(core, DOS_SW_RESET0);
+	amvdec_read_dos(core, DOS_SW_RESET0);
+
 	amvdec_write_dos_bits(core, POWER_CTL_VLD, BIT(9) | BIT(6));
 
 	workspace_offset = h264->workspace_paddr - WORKSPACE_BUF_OFFSET;
-- 
